<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phylakopi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phylakopi is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> town site on the north coast of Melos in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Occupied from about 2300 to 1100 BCE, it grew rich on the island's obsidian and on sea trade, and its remains show successive Cycladic, Minoan and Mycenaean influence, including frescoes such as the Flying Fish and a Mycenaean shrine with clay figures.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phylakopi.jpg" alt="Phylakopi" width="288" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phylakopi_flying_fish.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Phylakopi lies on the northern coast of Milos. Its occupation runs through the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, which has made it the type-site for several periods of the Aegean Bronze Age. The first settlement, founded on bedrock, was a small village with pottery of the Grotta-Pelos culture. Infants and fetuses were buried in pithoi beneath the floors of houses over several centuries of the Early and Middle Cycladic periods, while older members of the community were buried outside the settlement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Phylakopi I (2300-2000 BCE) was the first phase with substantial architecture. In Phylakopi II (2000-1550 BCE) the town flourished as a major power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a>, densely built in blocks of houses separated by long straight streets; its pottery included Dark Burnished ware, Cycladic white and the Melian bird jugs exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>. The Pillar Rooms Complex, about 21 by 13 metres, held frescoes of flying fish, lilies and a blue monkey; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colin-renfrew">Colin Renfrew</a> later redated it to the Late Cycladic period.</p><p>Phylakopi III (1550-1100 BCE) was built after the complete destruction of the second town, most likely by earthquake. In its first sub-phase Minoan forms prevailed, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> tablet fragment from the building called the Mansion suggests an administrative centre; some scholars have proposed Minoan political control. After the eruption of Thera Minoan influence receded and Mycenaean influence grew, with a megaron, a new fortification wall and Mycenaean pottery that almost replaced Cycladic styles, leading to the suggestion that the Mycenaeans conquered the settlement. The sanctuary, damaged in the Late Helladic IIIC period, was finally abandoned later in that period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The British School at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> first excavated the site from 1896 to 1899, with Duncan Mackenzie recording detailed stratigraphy and proposing the three phases. R. M. Dawkins and J. P. Droop re-excavated it in 1910-11, destroying much of Mackenzie's megaron without adequate record. Renfrew's excavations from 1974 revised the chronology and uncovered the sanctuary, with its terracotta figurines and stone altars. The flying fish frieze is displayed in the National Museum in Athens.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phylakopi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The settlements are known from excavated sites such as Chalandriani, <b>Phylakopi</b> and Skarkos.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cycladic-culture">Cycladic culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Within the citadel of Mycenae a group of shrines, the Cult Centre, contained clay figures of deities or worshippers, altars and wall paintings, and a shrine at <b>Phylakopi</b> on Melos held similar figures.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Melos was famous from the Neolithic for its obsidian and had an important Bronze Age town at <b>Phylakopi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/melos">Melos</a></li></ul></section>
